/// The overworld's chunks, as vanilla's `ServerChunkCache` and `ChunkMap` keep them: which load,
/// which are ready to send, and when they go (CAL-65).
///
/// `world.distance` says which chunks load, from the players' tickets and a joining player's
/// spawn ticket. Each chunk whose holder's level comes to 33, full, or below gets its chunk. A
/// world whose folder is open reads it from its region file, on the IO thread and the chunk
/// workers of `server.chunk_jobs`, and a chunk that wasn't saved is generated by the generator's
/// thread there (CAL-68), or flat in a flat world, as is every chunk of a world that reads
/// nothing; a flat chunk is sent from the flat world's one body, so it holds no chunk of its own.
/// A replay generates on its own thread what the recording's generator did. A chunk is ready to send once it ticks, as `getChunkToSend` has
/// it: its level is 32 or below and the 3×3 around it is loaded, as `prepareTickingChunk` waits
/// for. Its entities tick, which lets the dispatcher hand the next player tickets over, once the
/// 5×5 around it is loaded, as the entity ticking future waits for. A holder that unloads takes
/// its chunk with it.
///
/// Vanilla loads, generates and promotes chunks on worker threads, and when each is done is up
/// to them. Here what the IO thread reads comes back to the tick thread, which records it in the
/// input log and makes the chunk loaded in the order it came, then runs the distance manager
/// until it settles, so a replay has the same chunks at the same points (D178).
///
/// A world whose folder is open lights its chunks as vanilla's `ThreadedLevelLightEngine` does,
/// with `world.light`'s engines (CAL-76), on a thread of their own, `server.light_service`'s
/// (D264). Its holders load their chunks from level 34, the `initialize_light` status's, so each
/// full chunk has the chunks around it to be lit with. Light is told each chunk that comes, with
/// the light it was saved with, and each that goes, and which holders are full; it initializes
/// each chunk as it comes, and lights each whose holder is full once the 3×3 around it is
/// initialized. Only then is it full. What light did comes back a step at a time, which the
/// network records and hands to `lit`, so a replay runs light's steps on this thread, over the
/// same asks, and has the same chunks lit at the same points (D178). A chunk is sent with the
/// light light gives for it: the level wants a chunk's light once it's lit and a player's sender
/// comes to it, and keeps it, as it changes, until the chunk's sent to every player who wants
/// it, and asks for it again if it's wanted again (D266). A flat chunk whose neighbours are flat
/// too is sent from the flat world's body, since its light is the same. Light that changes once
/// a chunk is sent isn't sent: with no block changing, that's only at the edge of what's lit,
/// and it comes with block changes (D184).
///
/// A chunk sent with the engine's light is encoded once for every player who's to be sent it
/// (CAL-70). Its packet is kept while another player is still to be sent it, and they're sent
/// the same bytes, until its body, a body around it, or its light changes, when it's encoded
/// again. The last of them takes it, so a chunk only one player wants is never kept (D195).
/// Where packets are compressed, the first player's encoder compresses it on the way and the body
/// comes back to be kept in its place, so the others are sent it as it is (D196).
///
/// A view wider than vanilla's 32 has chunks the tickets don't reach: those more than 33 from
/// the player on an axis. Each player's are loaded as their sender comes to them, at most 64 at
/// once, and let go once they're sent, with their light as it was saved. A region's file is asked
/// which chunks it has first, so those it hasn't are sent flat at once, in a flat world, or
/// generated, and sent unlit, in one that generates. Two players who need the same far chunk
/// each read it (D179).
module server.level

